when we talk about contemporary turkey  we think of the longer career of the  current president erdogan but we will  come slowly to talk about his politics  first a little geography which never  hurts we are dealing with a nation in  the middle between southeastern europe  and western asia divided into two  peninsula regions eastern thrace and  anatolia border to the north west by  greece and bulgaria to the south by  syria and iraq and to the east by iran  armenia and georgia thanks to its  strategic location the country has  positioned itself as a bridge between  different cultures benefiting from  democratic influences from the old  continent and becoming a role model for  all middle eastern countries aspiring to  a synthesis of islam and the rule of law  now we are witnessing a reversal and the  recent authoritarian drift has greatly  slowed down the process of establishing  democratic principles we can say that  the beginning of these processes dates  back to the late 19th century when  competition with european powers led  some of the cultural elites within the  ottoman empire to push for modernization  a goal that according to some could be  achieved by maintaining a synthesis  between islamic faith and state  institutions but which according to  others including the famous mustafa  kemal required a clean break between  state and religion which had to be  limited to the private sphere of the  citizen after the 1923 elections that  saw the victory of the republican  people's party led by ataturk turkish  became the official language in place of  arabic the form of state was revised in  a republican key a head of state and the  prime minister appointed by the head of  state were established then within the  following year to solve the problem of  the difficult coexistence between  democracy and islam islam was erased  from the country's daily life by  abolishing the sultanate caliphate and  islamic courts this wasn't such a clean  exit however since despite the forced  secularization sunni islam continued to  remain very deeply rooted especially  among the rural and less educated  population also thanks to the influence  that religious brotherhoods have always  exerted on every aspect of society these  include the south oriented noxibendi to  which netmetinir bakan erdogan's  political father belonged and the his  met founded by fethullah gulen which  erdogan used to rise to power and with  which he came into open conflict during  the attempted cope of 2016. however what  were the highlights of the kemalist  reforms with the establishment of the  republic of turkey on october 29 1923  legislative and executive powers passed  into the hands of the assembly which was  responsible for electing the president  of the republic the head of the seoul  party for four-year terms the head of  state in turn appointed the president of  the council of ministers and the members  of the council itself completing the  picture where the stated independence of  the judiciary the recognition of the  formal equality of citizens before the  law and the granting of the active and  passive vote to women a milestone  achieved in 1934 long before several  european countries but it was in 1937  that the most important constitutional  reform took place the one that defined  the so-called six arrows of the turkish  state republican nationalist populist  statist reformist and secular ataturk's  death in 1938 and the shift from single  party to multiple political formations  caused by a number of adverse  circumstances including economic  backwardness compared to the west were  two of the main reasons that prevented  full democratic transition as the years  passed there was the emergence of more  liberal currents promoting private  initiative that met with the support of  the dissatisfied middle class and less  literate rural classes among these  gaining the most support was adnan  mender's democrat party which in the  1950 elections succeeded denouncing the  old single party and demolishing the  pillars of chemism measures such as the  call to prayer in arabic the opening of  many mosques and reading via state radio  of passages from the koran would allow  religious values to re-emerge in  contrast to the previous statist vision  the situation is also very similar in  contemporary turkey on the one hand the  aspiration for a secular and democratic  society as stated in the constitution  but on the other hand a deeply religious  society that struggles to recognize  itself in national democratic  institutions despite the new  government's good achievements including  membership in the international monetary  fund openness to both the west and arab  countries and decent economic growth  social tensions simmering in the  background erupted in the famous  istanbul pogrom of 1955 when a  right-wing nationalist group clashed  with a greek minority present in the  city's suburbs resulting in the deaths  of 30 people mandarin's party was  accused of taking an authoritarian turn  and planning the pogrom despite its  declared democratic openness so to  restore order and prevent a religious  drift in the country the army as the  guardian of the constitution seized the  power by force on may 27 1960. this date  frames the first of four military coups  in the period between 1960 and 1997  during which the secular organs of the  state represented by the military  several times came face to face against  the confessional parties  an important step is found in the second  coup of 1971 but especially the third in  1980 because they served to inaugurate  the so-called turkish islamic synthesis  that is the fusion of kemalism and sunni  islam in essence the military in order  to prevent the secularity of the state  from being compromised decided to come  to an agreement with the islamic  components of society becoming the  bridge for islam to officially penetrate  the institutions long story short in the  80s this was the situation after the  coup was over a consulting assembly  formed by the supreme military organ the  national security council and  constitutionalist experts was charged  with drafting the constitution it stated  the democratic and secular nature of the  turkish state confirmed the role of the  supreme military organ as the protector  of social order and expanded the powers  of the government and the president of  the republic the new electoral law then  set the threshold for entering  parliament at 10 percent so that in 1983  only three parties competed for seats  the people's party the nationalist party  and the motherland party the latter was  supported by the brotherhoods and  founded by turgot ozal who won 45  percent of the seats and became the  ruling party azzal's party succeeded in  embodying the turkish islamic synthesis  i mentioned bringing together the  military the brotherhoods and the small  and medium business class however  persistent economic backwardness  combined with the corruption and  inefficiency in the state began to  foster the emergence of the welfare  party founded by herbacan in 1987 and in  which a young erdogan was beginning to  make his mark the party wasn't driven by  any particular ideology but through a  team of economists tried to come up with  the best solutions to the problem of the  broken state finances it was an effort  that paid off as in the 1994 local  government elections the party wanted  more than 20 municipalities including  istanbul where erdogan was elected mayor  and in the july 1996 general elections  erbachan crowned his dream of becoming  prime minister but before long erbachan  would be way over his head as  conservative policies implemented by his  local administrators began to anger the  military these included plans to build  the mosque in istanbul staksim square a  place symbolic of the western way of  life and the desire to convert the  former saint sofia basilica into a  mosque another symbol of turkish secular  tradition since it had been turned into  a museum in 1934 by the will of bataturk  himself in addition contacts between the  welfare party and other middle eastern  islamic groups such as the palestinian  hamas the muslim brotherhood in egypt  and the algerian islamic salvation front  were putting the party in a bad light in  the eyes of the military and here we  come to the fourth coup of 1997 also  called the postmodern coupe because it  was implemented without bloodshed with  irbachan banned from political life  accused of stealing public funds and  inciting religious hate the welfare  party was dismantled and in its place  was born the following year the virtue  party into which the young erdogan  joined even though shortly before he had  been sentenced to 10 months in prison  and given his excellent record as mayor  of istanbul it's not surprising that  much of the population considered his  detention and injustice that would have  made him even more charming anyway of  the originally planned 10 months erdogan  served only four since in 2001 the  turkish constitutional court granted  amnesty to virtue party members who had  been banned from public office at that  point the party split into two fronts  the conservative wing of the islamic  metrics and the wing of the so-called  innovators the young moderate reformists  of which erdogan became the leader  founding the justice and development  party on august 14 of that year in the  november 3 2002 elections the akp became  the first party with 34.3 percent of the  vote but for some reasons that i won't  go into here erdogan had to lead from  behind the scenes letting his loyal  right-hand man abdullah ghoul replace  him as prime minister the akp on the  strength of its policy proposals aimed  at reviving the country's economic  situation hit by the 2001 crisis and  rising inflation would set up a muslim  government inspired by european  democracies but with the sun islamic  emphasis as ghul himself said although  some of us are religiously oriented  individuals we don't want to be defined  as a religious party we want to prove  that a muslim-majority country can be  democratic transparent and compatible  with the modern world major achievements  in the early 2000s including economic  growth of five percent annually  diversification of foreign trade and an  aid program for the poorer sections of  the population projected turkey into the  role of original power making it a  viable candidate for entry into the  european union so much so that  negotiations reopened as early as  october 2005. dear erdogan if last year  you hadn't confined ursula fonder lion  to that little couch maybe your flag  would also be in brussels today  jokes aside internally the reform of the  national security council was important  remember the supreme military organ  which was downgraded to an advisory  organ thus lessening the military's grip  on institutions in short a series of  achievements that led the akp to prevail  in the july 22 2007 elections with as  many as  46.6 percent of votes in favor  corresponding to 341 seats but on  balance the most important achievement  for the turkish leader was that of  having succeeded in getting abdullah  ghul elected to the position of  president of the republic which had in  fact consolidated his power over the  opposition the situation that had been  created however was a harbinger of a  dangerous authoritarian drift because  the government would be able to push  through laws bordering on constitutional  legitimacy without the head of state  casting any kind of veto starting in  2008 a number of events threatened to  compromise the prime minister's career  forever first and foremost the law  restoring the veil for women in  universities almost caused the party  closure for anti-like activities  according to the dossier submitted by  the chief prosecutor of the turkish  supreme court the rescue came when the  constitutional court called to rule on  the issue initially declared the law  contrary to the constitution but a few  months later reversed itself on public  interest grounds then it was the turn of  the prime minister's very strong  reaction against the members of the  organicon an alleged chemist-style  secret organization composed mainly of  senior army officials established with  the aim of overthrowing the government  and seizing power in a coup however an  affair that remains unclear to this day  with many accusing erdogan himself of  organizing everything to dispose of his  political opponents a 2016 ruling would  partially confirm these fears by  releasing most of those convicted  finally it was in may 2013 that there  was a harsh crackdown on  environmentalist protests against the  construction of a shopping mall in the  green area of genji park near taxing  square on that occasion police  intervened against the protesters using  tear gas and water cannons the media and  newspapers loyal to the government tried  to cover up the news but this caused the  event to resonate even more through  social networks such as facebook and  twitter which were blacked out anyway  the turbulent situation that had been  created was not enough to undermine the  popularity of erdogan who have increased  the three-term limit and unable to run  again for the leadership of the  government run for the presidency of the  republic in 2014 and one with 52 of the  votes in favor at that point the new  turkey project desired by the president  to require the annihilating the last  internal opponent still capable of  challenging him i'm referring to that  fetullah gulen who had long ago helped  him get rid of the catalysts and who  after the gezi park affair had instead  obstructed him his brotherhood let's  remember that he's met was from them on  branded as terrorists and targeted by  government forces the democratic values  that had brought the ruling party to  prominence back in 2002 had been  betrayed with a society sharply divided  between supporters and opponents of the  regime and the president with little  tolerance for his enemies frequent was  the use of article 299 of the turkish  panel code which provided for  imprisonment for all those who offended  the president of the republic a measure  that among other things in 2021 was  declared by the european court of human  rights to be detrimental to freedom of  expression the authoritarian drift was  so obvious that even within erdogan's  own party scorched earth had been made a  few years earlier the genji park affair  had caused the removal of loyalist  ghouls and in may 2016 trusted prime  minister ahmet tabotogul was also  removed due to differences in political  views and replaced by bin ali ildrim  boiling tensions erupted on that friday  evening july 15 2016 when a reporter  from state tv trt began reading a  lengthy statement from the coupe  plotters announcing the seizure of power  at the same time the military had  occupied the southern bridges over the  bosphorus in istanbul and two jets flew  over the city spreading terror among the  population erdogan declared in a live  video feed made from his smartphone that  he had survived the attack and called  for citizens to take to the streets to  defend democracy it was hours of terror  in ankara the parliamentary headquarters  was hard hit by the air force and  reduced to a pile of rubble but by the  next morning it was clear that the coup  had failed as army units still loyal to  the president managed to restore the  status quo even today it's difficult to  say who organized the coup at first  aronstein claimed that it was some  departments of the first and second  armies that carried it out along with  the support of elements of the air force  navy and coast guard another article by  political scientists vanta cornell  asserted instead that had been the work  of an unlikely alliance between old  style chemists and gulenists anyway the  casualties were about 240 civilians and  a few military personnel nothing  compared to those who die or be forced  to flee because of the government's  harsh reaction one thing is certain that  was indeed the golden opportunity to  wipe out all the main opponents and more  in fact gulen was blamed as the  architect of the coup backed by the cia  quick parenthesis remember that the last  period of the war in syria had brought  relations between russia and turkey  closer together meeting with a lot of  criticism from the united states after  that a state of emergency was declared  and not only the sectors of the military  and civil service involved in the coup  were affected but also those with whom  in the previous 15 years of rule the  party had had to come to terms in order  to secure power i leave you with the  detailed account of the various arrests  and dismissals that followed at that  point this would have been the icing on  the cake to lay hands on the  constitution with reform and move the  country toward the strong  presidentialism inspired by the french  model the group had caused the main  opposition forces to express solidarity  with the president and show willingness  to consider their proposed  constitutional reform however the quorum  needed to pass the amendments wasn't  reached and it was appropriate to go  through a referendum on april 16 2017  erdogan won with just  51.41  a victory that among other things was  criticized both by domestic oppositions  which accused the government of  electoral fraud and by the organization  for security and cooperation in europe  and the council of europe it was an  outcome that reflected the deep internal  rift in the country where erdogan's  legitimacy was slowly being challenged  by a general climate of protest over  arrests and repression by the  authorities that went far beyond those  belonging to fetal aguilance  organization it was very difficult for  the oppositions to work because at the  first mistake they risked being branded  as gulenists which over time has become  synonymous with terrorists today's  turkey which will celebrate the 100th  anniversary of the birth of the republic  in 2023 has its leadership a president  far away from the figure the father of  the turks envisioned of the catalyst the  six arrows very few remain intact and  among them it's clear that secularism is  no longer among the features of the  turkish state but still the political  involution we are witnessing has been  countered by economic growth such that  the country has become a major regional  power that was at least until the most  recent monetary policies led to the  devaluation of the turkish lira  culminating in the currency crisis of  2021 and the consequent rise in  inflation generating the risk of  stagnation in the economy due to the  rising cost of living and falling  internal demand all of this would also  impact the degree of discontent so much  so that impulse approval ratings toward  the erdogan's actions would have dropped  from 55.8 percent in march 2020 to 38.6  at the end of 2021 the lowest percentage  in recent years even lower than in early  2019 when dissatisfaction with the  performance of the economy had caused  the erdogan to lose control of istanbul  and ankara in local elections but it's  good to stop here for today i thank you  all for your attention and i will see  you in the next video ciao

